\description{
Check values in a period column, separately within groups when required.
Diagnostics return a flat report; assertions stop at the first failing
group and otherwise return the original data frame invisibly.
}
\section{Grouping}{

If \code{by} is omitted, use existing \code{\link[dplyr:group_by]{dplyr::group_by()}} groups, or check all
rows together for ungrouped input. An explicitly supplied \code{by} replaces
existing groups. Explicit \code{by = NULL} or \code{character(0)} checks all rows
together, even for grouped input. Column arguments use
\link[tidyselect:language]{tidyselect} semantics, including bare names,
ranges, and selection helpers. Renaming columns is not supported.

Groups appear in order of first observation. Missing keys match missing
keys. Unused grouping-factor levels and empty groups are ignored.
With omitted \code{by}, rowwise tibbles are checked one row at a time,
retaining any identifier columns as report keys. Assertions preserve
the original grouping, including when \code{by} overrides the checks.
}

\section{Values and levels}{

Only observed values are checked; unused label-factor levels are ignored.
Actual \code{NA} observations are included. Repeated interval labels within
a group fail \code{no_overlap}; repeats across groups do not interact.
To check a factor's declared classification, including unused levels,
use \code{\link[=period_diagnose_levels]{period_diagnose_levels()}} or \code{\link[=period_assert_levels]{period_assert_levels()}}.

\code{no_gap} checks coverage between the earliest and latest observed
intervals. It does not require groups to have the same span or detect
absent groups or missing end categories. Other checks retain the rules
described in \code{\link[=period_diagnose_levels]{period_diagnose_levels()}}.

For column names stored in character vectors, use
\code{\link[tidyselect:all_of]{tidyselect::all_of()}} or \code{\link[tidyselect:any_of]{tidyselect::any_of()}}. A wrapper accepting
selection expressions can forward them with \code{{{ }}}. To preserve inferred
grouping, a wrapper must omit \code{by} when its own \code{by} is omitted.
}

\section{Empty inputs}{

With no grouping, a zero-row frame represents one empty set of values:
absence (\code{no_}) and validity checks pass, and presence (\code{has_}) checks
fail. With grouping and no rows there are no observed groups, so the
report is empty and assertions pass. With no checks requested, reports
have zero rows; input validation and parsing still apply.
}

\section{Interpretation messages}{

Default multi-year interpretation advice is emitted at most once per
call, considering observed labels rather than unused factor levels.
}

\section{Rules for interpreting inputs}{


\strong{Single-year}

Labels for single-year periods sometimes refer to
the lower limits of periods, and sometimes
refer to upper limits. For instance, the label \code{"2020"}
is sometimes used to refer to the period that
starts at midnight on 1 January 2020
and ends at midnight on 1 January 2021,
and is sometimes used to refer to the
period that starts at midnight on 1 July 2019
and ends at midnight on 1 July 2020.

The \code{interpret_single} argument can be used to distinguish
between the two conventions:\tabular{lll}{
   \code{interpret_single} \tab \emph{Rule} \tab \emph{Example} \cr
   \code{"lower"} \tab \verb{"a" -> [a,a+1)} \tab \verb{"2020" -> [2020,2021)} \cr
   \code{"upper"} \tab \verb{"a" -> [a-1,a)} \tab \verb{"2020" -> [2019,2020)} \cr
}


\strong{Multi-year}

In labels for multi-year periods, the second number is
sometimes the upper limit of the period, and sometimes
the upper limit minus 1. For instance, the period
that starts at midnight on 1 January 2020 and ends
at midnight on 1 January 2025 is sometimes labelled
\code{"2020-2025"} and is sometimes labelled \code{"2020-2024"}.

The \code{interpret_multi} argument can be used to distinguish
between the two conventions:\tabular{lll}{
   \code{interpret_multi} \tab \emph{Rule} \tab \emph{Example} \cr
   \code{"at_upper"} \tab \verb{"a-b" -> [a,b)} \tab \verb{"2020-2025" -> [2020,2025)} \cr
   \code{"below_upper"} \tab \verb{"a-b" -> [a,b+1)} \tab \verb{"2020-2024" -> [2020,2025)} \cr
}


If \code{interpret_multi} is \code{NULL} (the default), then multi-year
labels are interpreted using the \code{"at_upper"} convention.
If, however, the \code{"at_upper"} convention
leads to intervals with
widths 4, 9, 14, \dots, then \pkg{agetime} emits a message.
Supplying an explicit value for \code{interpret_multi}
silences this message.

\strong{Open}

A period that is open on the left has no lower limit,
and a period that is open on the right has no upper limit.
Labels for open periods are not affected by \code{interpret_single}
or \code{interpret_multi}.

Some examples of open periods:\tabular{ll}{
   \emph{Label} \tab \emph{Interval} \cr
   \code{"<2000"}, \code{"before 2000"}, \code{"under 2000"} \tab \verb{(-Inf,2000)} \cr
   \code{"2000 or earlier"}, \code{"up to 2000"} \tab \verb{(-Inf,2001)} \cr
   \code{"2000+"}, \code{"2000 or more"}, \code{"2000 or later"} \tab \verb{[2000,Inf)} \cr
}


Labels starting with \code{"-"}, such as \code{"-2000"}, are not accepted.
}

